       (notes-statement "If R is a field, the n x n elementary column matrices generate the group of invertible matrices in M_n(R)."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.15")
       (section "3.5" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A permutation of the indeterminates is a ring automorphism of R[x_1,...,x_n]")
       (names var-permute-is-ring-iso multi-index-reindex-is-ring-iso monalg-pullback-iso mpoly-is-ring mpoly-is-commutative-ring multi-index-monoid-is-comm-monoid)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "R[x_1, ..., x_n] is MPOLY(R, n) = MONALG(R, MULTI-INDEX-MONOID([1, n])), the multi-indices being the functions [1, n] -> NN under pointwise addition. sigma is a bijection of [1, n] (the notes' definition of a permutation), not a member of PERMUTATIONS(n), which is defined on ORD-SEGMENT(n). sigma . p is VAR-PERMUTE(R, n, sigma, p), whose coefficient at beta is p(beta o sigma); isomorphism is IS-ISO-RING (a ring hom and a bijection of the carriers). Proven for any ring R; the notes take R commutative, and MPOLY(R, n) is commutative when R is (mpoly-is-commutative-ring). The proof is the general monalg-pullback-iso: a monoid automorphism of M induces a ring automorphism of MONALG(A, M).")
       (notes-statement "For a permutation sigma of the indeterminates, p |-> sigma . p is an isomorphism of the ring R[x_1, x_2, ..., x_n]."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.18")
       (section "3.5" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "det is multilinear and alternating, and det of the transpose equals det")
       (names det-row-linear det-col-linear det-swap-rows det-swap-cols det-alternating-rows det-equal-cols-zero det-transpose)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 32): PROVEN in full over any commutative ring: TRANSPOSE is defined entrywise (structure-library/transpose.scm, the user's decision), det-col-linear, det-swap-cols and det-equal-cols-zero are the column clauses, and det-transpose (det(A^t) = det A) follows from the uniqueness theorem det-alternating-form since X |-> det(X^t) is alternating n-linear in the rows. UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 25): the ROW clauses are PROVEN over any commutative ring (theorem-library/det-rows.scm): det-row-linear (linear in each row), det-swap-rows (interchanging two rows negates det), and det-alternating-rows (two equal rows give 0) is now a THEOREM, its support retired; the proof from the first-row cofactor definition needs a second level of expansion (rows 1 and 2 equal: a double sum cancelling in pairs, no division by 2, so characteristic 2 is covered). The COLUMN clauses and det(A^t) = det A wait on a TRANSPOSE, which is a definition (proposed in BATCH25-REPORTS.md, not added). EARLIER NOTE: Only one of the three claims exists, and in a different form.  det-alternating-rows is ASSERTED (warrant reference) and says that two EQUAL rows force det = zero(r) -- not that interchanging two rows changes the sign.  Linearity in each row and in each column is absent.  det(A^t) = det(A) is absent and unstatable: the library has NO matrix transpose operation.  Ring hypothesis agrees (is-commutative-ring).  Note that the library's det is DEFINED by cofactor expansion along the first row, not by the notes' sum over permutations, so even the definitions are only provably equal in principle.")
       (notes-statement "det A is linear in each column and each row; interchanging two distinct rows or two distinct columns yields B with det A = - det B; and det(A^t) = det A."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.19")
       (section "3.5" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Determinant under an elementary column operation")
       (names det-elem-f-col det-elem-g-col det-elem-h-col det-mul-elem-f-col det-mul-elem-g-col det-mul-elem-h-col)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 32): PROVEN (theorem-library/det-transpose.scm), each in one line from det-multiplicative and the determinants of the elementary matrices; the H clause is stated for every scalar r of the ring, not only a unit. The determinant of an elementary matrix is computed nowhere in the library -- there is no det of elem-f, elem-g or elem-h, and no det of a diagonal matrix from which det H could be read.  The asserted det-multiplicative would reduce these to those three values, which are missing.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_n(R): det(A F^col[k,l]) = - det A = det A det F^col[k,l]; det(A G^col[r,k,l]) = det A = det A det G^col[r,k,l]; det(A H^col[r,k]) = r det A = det A det H^col[r,k]."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.20")
       (section "3.5" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "det(AV) = det A det V for invertible V over a field")
       (names det-mul-invertible det-multiplicative)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 26-B): PROVEN as a corollary of 3.27 (det-mul-invertible: over a field, V invertible, det(AV) = det A det V). EARLIER NOTE: UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 25): det(EA) = det E det A is PROVEN for each elementary E (theorem-library/det-mul.scm), but the tree has no theorem that an invertible matrix over a field is a product of elementary matrices (and no finite product of matrices to state it with), so this corollary and det-multiplicative remain asserted. The one lemma that proves 3.27 for EVERY commutative ring, and 3.20 with it, is Hoffman-Kunze 5.3 Theorem 2: an alternating n-linear D satisfies D(A) = det(A) D(I); with row linearity and alternation now proven it is about one file. EARLIER NOTE: The library asserts the UNRESTRICTED multiplicativity -- any commutative ring, any two square matrices of the same size, no invertibility and no field -- which subsumes this corollary.  It carries warrant reference, i.e. it is trusted, not proven.")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field and V in M_n(R) is invertible, then det(A V) = det A det V."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.22")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Expansion by minors along a row and along a column")
       (names det-expand-row det-expand-col)
       (definitions det-cofactor)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 32): the COLUMN half is PROVEN, det-expand-col (for each j, det A = sum_i (-1)^(i+j) a_ij det A[i,j]), by transposing det-expand-row with (minor of A^t at (j,i)) = (minor of A at (i,j))^t. UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 25): the ROW half is PROVEN: det-expand-row expands along any row i with the sign (-1)^(i+j). The column half waits on the transpose. EARLIER NOTE: The library DEFINES det by the cofactor expansion along the FIRST ROW: det-cofactor is the defining equation for det(r, succ(n), a), not a theorem.  So the notes' statement is available only at i = 1, and only as a definition; the expansion along an arbitrary row i, and the column expansion, are nowhere stated.  The library's minor(s,r,c,n) is the notes' A[i,j].  The sign (-1)^{i+j} appears in det-cofactor as mpow of neg(one(r)) to succ(j), the i = 1 specialisation.")
       (notes-statement "For each i, det A = sum_j (-1)^{i+j} a_{ij} det(A[i,j]); and for each j, det A = sum_i (-1)^{i+j} a_{ij} det(A[i,j])."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.23")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries")
       (names det-lower-triangular det-upper-triangular)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "IS-LOWER-TRIANGULAR and IS-UPPER-TRIANGULAR are the predicates (entries above, respectively below, the diagonal are zero); the product of the diagonal is PROD-RING over [1, n]. Stated over a commutative ring. The induction runs through the last row or column instead of a_11, and the upper triangular case is proved directly, not by transposition.")
       (notes-statement "If A is lower triangular then det A is the product of the diagonal entries; likewise for upper triangular A."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.25")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The formal adjoint satisfies adj(A) A = det(A) I")
       (names adj-mul mul-adj cramer-inverse)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "ADJ(R, n, A) is the adjoint of an (n+1)-square matrix (the size argument is n, the convention of DET's recursion), so the statement covers sizes >= 1 only. Stated over a commutative ring. The inverse formula is cramer-inverse: for b in CARR R with det(A) b = 1, b adj(A) is a two-sided inverse of A.")
       (notes-statement "For any n x n matrix A, the formal adjoint with entries (-1)^{i+j} det(A[j,i]) satisfies adj(A) A = det(A) I; hence A^{-1} = det(A)^{-1} adj(A)."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.26")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Over a field, invertible iff the determinant is nonzero")
       (names invertible-iff-det-nonzero)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "R is IS-FIELD-RING (a commutative ring with 1 /= 0 in which every nonzero element has an inverse).")
       (notes-statement "If R is a field, A in M_n(R) is invertible if and only if det A /= 0."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.27")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "det is multiplicative")
       (names det-multiplicative det-alternating-form)
       (home-elsewhere det-multiplicative)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 26-B): PROVEN for every commutative ring, theorem-library/det-multiplicative.scm: X |-> det(XQ) is linear in each row and zero on two equal rows, and Hoffman-Kunze 5.3 Theorem 2 (det-alternating-form: such a D is det(A) D(I)) is proven by induction on n without permutations or signs; the support is retired. EARLIER NOTE: Same statement, same hypothesis (is-commutative-ring, both matrices n x n), but ASSERTED with warrant reference -- it is trusted surface, never proven.  It is one of only two determinant facts on the debt bundle (with det-alternating-rows).")
       (notes-statement "det(A B) = det A det B for all A, B in M_n(R), R a commutative ring."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.29")
       (section "3.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Behaviour of the sigma-th order subdeterminants under F and G operations")
       (names sdet-col-swap sdet-row-swap sdet-col-add sdet-row-add sdet-col-scale sdet-row-scale)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "SDET(R, m, n, A, s) is the set of determinants of SUBMATRIX(A, s, p, q) for INJECTIVE index maps p, q, where the notes use increasing ones; it is the notes' sdet_sigma together with the negatives of its elements, so 'identical up to sign' under an F operation becomes equality of the two sets. Stated over a commutative ring; the H companions sdet-col-scale and sdet-row-scale are not in the notes.")
       (notes-statement "If B comes from A by an F row or column operation then sdet_sigma B = sdet_sigma A up to sign; if by a G operation then every element of sdet_sigma B has the form a + s a' with a, a' in sdet_sigma A and s = 0 or s = +/- r."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.31")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The gcd of the sigma-th subdeterminants is an invariant over Z")
       (names sdet-gcd-invariant sdet-divisor-equiv)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "SDET uses injective index maps where the notes use increasing ones (see 3.29); no gcd changes. A -> B is ELEM-EQUIV(ZZ-RING, m, n, A, B): B = P A Q with P, Q in ELEM-GEN. A gcd of a set is ZZ-IS-GCD-SET(S, d), and the conclusion is d = e or d = -e for any gcd d of SDET(A) and e of SDET(B). The notes' ideal argument is replaced by its divisor form.")
       (notes-statement "If A, B in M_{m x n}(Z) and A -> B by elementary F, G row and column operations over Z, then gcd sdet_sigma A = +/- gcd sdet_sigma B."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.36")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Integer normal form: existence and uniqueness of the diagonal form")
       (names integer-normal-form integer-normal-form-exists diagonal-normal-form-unique diagonal-normal-form-unique-invertible diagonal-chain-sdet-gcd)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The relation ~ is ELEM-EQUIV(ZZ-RING, m, n, A, B): B = P A Q with P, Q in ELEM-GEN (over Z the row and the column elementary matrices form one set).  Existence (integer-normal-form-exists) gives a diagonal B with non-negative entries and the divisibility chain; uniqueness (diagonal-normal-form-unique, and diagonal-normal-form-unique-invertible for U D V with U, V invertible over Z) goes through diagonal-chain-sdet-gcd: b_11 ... b_ss is a gcd of the s-th subdeterminants.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_{m x n}(Z) there is a UNIQUE B ~ A with (1) nonzero entries only on the diagonal, (2) all entries non-negative, (3) b_ii divides b_jj for j >= i."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.37")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The reducing matrices U and V are products of elementary matrices")
       (names integer-normal-form-elementary)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "U and V lie in ELEM-GEN(ZZ-RING, -): over Z the row and the column elementary matrices generate one set, so the notes' distinction between products of column and of row elementary matrices disappears.")
       (notes-statement "For A in M_{m x n}(Z) there are U in M_m(Z), V in M_n(Z) with U A V in the normal form of 3.36, U a product of column elementary matrices and V a product of row elementary matrices; in particular both are invertible over Z."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.38")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "An invertible integer matrix is a product of elementary matrices")
       (names zz-invertible-iff-elementary-product euclidean-invertible-iff-elem-gen euclidean-unit-det-elem-gen elem-gen-det-unit)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "U = P Q with P, Q in ELEM-GEN(ZZ-RING, n); over ZZ the column and the row elementary matrices form one set. Proved directly by reduction on the whole matrix, through the theorem for a euclidean ring, not from 3.37.")
       (notes-statement "U in M_m(Z) is invertible over Z if and only if it is a product U V with U a product of column elementary matrices and V a product of row elementary matrices."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.40")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "An invertible change of sequence preserves generation and freeness")
       (names generates-transport free-transport spans-transport)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library's two statements are ONE-WAY implications, not the notes' iff: generates(md,n,u) implies generates(md,n,matact(md,pm,u)), and rel-free(md,m,v) implies rel-free(md,m,matact(md,qm,v)).  The converses (which follow by applying the same theorems to the inverse matrix) are not installed.  Setting: a module md over any ring with the transport matrix invertible over scal(md); the notes' E is an ABELIAN GROUP and U is over Z, and the library has NO abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view (zz-act exists as an integer action, but no module structure is built from it), so the notes' own case is not an instance of the library statement.  spans-transport is the submodule version, not in the notes.")
       (notes-statement "If U in M_n^x(Z), then u = <u_1,...,u_n> generates E if and only if U . u generates E, and u is relation-free if and only if U . u is."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.41")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A relation-free sequence is no longer than a generating sequence")
       (names free-length-le-generators free-generating-length-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Both sentences are proven for a module over a EUCLIDEAN scalar ring (an abelian group is the case SCAL = ZZ-RING).  The second sentence is free-generating-length-unique: two free generating sequences have equal lengths.  Guards the notes do not carry: n, m in nn, and both sequences are column matrices u in mat(n,1,vec(md)), v in mat(m,1,vec(md)).")
       (notes-statement "If E is generated by n generators then any relation-free sequence has at most n elements; in particular all free generating sequences of an abelian group have the same length."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.43")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "GL_n(Z) acts freely and transitively on the free generating sequences")
       (names free-generating-action-free free-generating-action-stabilizer free-generating-action-transitive free-generating-action-closed)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven for a module over any ring; the action is matact.  The group M_n^x(Z) is not formed as a group: freeness is stated as A . u = u implies A = I for an invertible A and a free generating u, transitivity as the existence of an invertible A with v = A . u, closure as A . u free generating again.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a free abelian group of rank n, the action (A, u) |-> A . u of M_n^x(Z) on free generating sequences of E is free and transitive."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.44")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Adapted basis for a subgroup, with the divisibility chain")
       (names principal-sequence-exists principal-sequence-exists-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for a module md with SCAL(md) = ZZ-RING (the notes' free abelian group of rank n), F a submodule; the coefficients b_i are the diagonal of the matrix dm in IS-PRINCIPAL-SEQUENCE(md, n, sm, es, dm).  Invariance is 3.48.")
       (notes-statement "If E is free abelian of rank n then for any subgroup F there are free generators e_1,...,e_n of E and non-negative integers b_1,...,b_n with b_i dividing b_{i+1} such that b_1 e_1, ..., b_n e_n generate F; the b_i are invariants of F."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.45")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The norm N_F(E): the absolute determinant is basis-independent")
       (names norm-independent change-of-basis-det-unit)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for F of rank n (so that A is square), with SCAL(md) = ZZ-RING; change-of-basis-det-unit holds over any commutative ring.  N_E(F) is defined as SUBMODULE-NORM(md, sm): |det am| for any IS-NORM-DATA presentation (free generating xs of E, relation-free ys spanning F, both of length n, ys = am . xs), else 0 (submodule-norm-value, submodule-norm-zero, norm-data-det-unique).")
       (notes-statement "If F is contained in E, with free generating sets v, u and v = A . u, then |det A| does not depend on the choice of v and u."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.46")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A subgroup of a free abelian group of rank n is free of rank at most n")
       (names submodule-of-free-is-free)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN modulo 0 (theorem-library/rake-asm-b4.scm, 2026-10-12).  The library form is for a submodule sm of a module md over a EUCLIDEAN scalar ring, with a generating AND rel-free column family u of length n; the conclusion is a k <= n and a w in mat(k,1,vec(md)) with spans(md,k,w,sm) and rel-free(md,k,w) -- the notes' free of rank <= n, granted the missing abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view.")
       (notes-statement "Any subgroup of a free abelian group of rank n is free of rank at most n."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.47")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A subgroup of an n-generated group is generated by at most n elements")
       (names submodule-fg spans-submodule-fg)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "This is the library's headline linear-algebra result, proven and billing modulo 0 since 2026-09-17.  Differences: it is stated for a MODULE over a euclidean ring (the notes' abelian group is not an instance, the abelian-group-as-ZZ-module view being absent), and its conclusion is a column family w in mat(k,1,vec(md)) with k <= n that SPANS the submodule.  spans-submodule-fg is the strictly more general form the descent actually needs: a submodule of a submodule spanned by n elements is spanned by k <= n.  The notes' own proof of this corollary (through Z^n and 3.44) is CIRCULAR -- Smith form consumes a finite generating set for F and cannot produce one -- so the library proves it directly, by descent on the ideal of last coefficients; the \\iffalse'd induction at tex:1651 is load-bearing in the notes.")
       (notes-statement "Any subgroup F of a group E generated by n elements is generated by a set of n elements or fewer."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.48")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the principal sequence of a subgroup")
       (names principal-sequence-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The principal sequence is the diagonal of the matrix dm in IS-PRINCIPAL-SEQUENCE(md, n, sm, es, dm).  The proof goes through diagonal-right-factor-sdet-divisible and the invariance of the subdeterminant gcds (3.31), not through bases of F, and needs no Cauchy-Binet.")
       (notes-statement "The principal sequence b of a subgroup F of E is uniquely determined."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.49")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The quotient E/F for a diagonal subgroup")
       (names quotient-by-diagonal-subgroup first-isomorphism-module)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "All n summands are written Z/(b_i), Z/(0) standing for Z, so the notes' split into k cyclic summands and Z^{n-k} is one direct sum.  The quotient is QUOTIENT-MODULE(md, sm); first-isomorphism-module is the general first isomorphism theorem for modules.")
       (notes-statement "If E is freely generated by e_1,...,e_n, the a_1,...,a_k are non-zero and F is generated by a_1 e_1,...,a_k e_k, then E/F is isomorphic to Z/(a_1) + ... + Z/(a_k) + Z^{n-k}."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.50")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Structure of a finitely generated abelian group")
       (names fg-abelian-group-structure-exists fg-abelian-group-structure-unique cyclic-sum-iso-core)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "E is a ZZ-module generated by n elements.  The invariant factors are normalised a_i >= 0 and a_i /= 1, Z/(0) standing for Z, with l <= n; uniqueness is equality of the sequences, not up to sign.  The isomorphism is stated as existing: every homomorphism out of the sum is the notes' map induced by the w_i.  Uniqueness goes through Schanuel's lemma (schanuel-iso) and 3.48.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a finitely generated abelian group there are w_1,...,w_l in E and non-negative a_1,...,a_l with a_i dividing a_j for i <= j such that (x_1,...,x_l) |-> sum x_i w_i induces an isomorphism Z/(a_1) + ... + Z/(a_l) -> E; the sequence a_1,...,a_l is unique up to sign."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.51")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Every finitely generated abelian group is a direct sum of cyclic groups")
       (names fg-abelian-group-cyclic-decomposition cyclic-sum-iso-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "m_k >= 0 and m_k /= 1 replace the notes' m_k > 0, which would exclude the free part; uniqueness means equal lengths and equal sequences.")
       (notes-statement "Every finitely generated abelian group is a finite direct sum Z/(m_1) + ... + Z/(m_l) with m_k > 0 and m_k dividing m_{k+1}; the representation is unique."))

(entry (doc "linear-algebra")
       (ref "3.52")
       (section "3.8" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The cardinality of E/F is the norm N_E(F)")
       (names quotient-card-norm cyclic-dsum-card principal-sequence-of-presentation dsum-card zz-mod-card card-cartesian)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "E is a module md with SCAL(md) = ZZ-RING and a free generating sequence of length n; F = sm is a submodule.  N_E(F) is SUBMODULE-NORM(md, sm): |det A| when F has rank n, else 0.  card(E/F) is CARD(VEC(QUOTIENT-MODULE(md, sm))).  The proof goes through 3.44, 3.45, 3.49, card(Z/(b)) = b and the product formula for a finite direct sum; the freeness of E is not used beyond its generating sequence.")
       (notes-statement "If E is a finitely generated free abelian group, F a subgroup and N_E(F) /= 0, then card(E/F) = N_E(F)."))

;;; ===== complex-analysis =====
;;; complex-analysis.sexp -- MAPPING agent MT-4, 2026-09-21.
;;; Document: /home/ubuntu/docs/complex-analysis.pdf, "Complex Analysis", F. Javier Thayer.
;;; Scope (by the user's decision): chapter 1 sections 1.1, 1.2, 1.3, 1.5, 1.6 (section 1.4
;;; SKIPPED), chapter 2 in full (2.1, 2.2, 2.3), and section 3.1 only.
;;; Numbered results of kind Remark or Definition are not listed (1.1, 1.4, 1.6, 1.8, 1.10,
;;; 1.13, 1.17, 1.23, 2.1, 2.6, 2.11); Prop 1.12 and Lemma 1.14 lie in the skipped 1.4.
;;; Library side read off reference/THEOREMS.md (proven / PSS / axiom / definition /
;;; assertion sections), reference/PSS.md, reference/DEFINITIONS.md; `proven' confirmed by
;;; grepping (qed 'NAME) under theorem-library/.
;;; Added 2026-10-15 (agents CA-AC and CA-5, 2026-10-14; merged into the library 2026-10-15).
;;; The OUTLINE of complex-analysis: chapters that hold an entry, with every section the notes have.
;;; Titles marked `prefilled' were read off the notes by pdftotext (BOOK-GEN, 2026-11-12); review
;;; and delete the mark.  Read by library-book.scm; ignored by the major-theorems list.
