(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.2")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Uniqueness of the least upper bound")
       (names rr-lub-unique)
       (home-elsewhere rr-lub-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "A least upper bound is an RR-UPPER-BOUND that is <= every upper bound (Definition 1.8); no guard on the set. (calculus 1.9 and complex-analysis 1.2 share this statement.)")
       (notes-statement "Any S subset of R has at most one least upper bound."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.3")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "C as 2x2 real matrices")
       (names cc-matrix-representation cc-matrix-rep-bijection cc-matrix-of-mul)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "CC-MATRICES is a set of real 2x2 matrices, not a ring structure; 'algebraic isomorphism' is stated as Phi in BIJECTION(CC, CC-MATRICES) together with the laws for +, x, 1 and real multiples (MATADD, MATMUL, IDENTMAT, MATSCALE over the ring view of RR-NORMED-FIELD). Sums and bijectivity, omitted in the notes, are proven.")
       (notes-statement "Phi : a + b i |-> [[a, b], [-b, a]] is an algebraic isomorphism from C onto the set of 2x2 real matrices of that form."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.5")
       (section "1.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Unions and finite intersections of open sets")
       (names union-of-opens-open inter-of-finite-opens-open)
       (home-elsewhere union-of-opens-open inter-of-finite-opens-open)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The finite family is a set c with CARD(c) in NN, inhabited, all members open; the intersection is INTERSECTION-OF(c). The union half is unchanged.")
       (notes-statement "The union of any family of open sets is open. The intersection of finitely many open sets is open."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.7")
       (section "1.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Monotone convergence for real sequences")
       (names monotone-converges-to-sup monotone-converges-to-inf-literal)
       (home-elsewhere monotone-converges-to-sup monotone-converges-to-inf-literal)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "sup_n x_n is SUP of the range {y in RR : y = f(k)}; inf_n x_n is INF of the range (INF defined as -SUP of the negated set, proven to be the glb). Monotonicity in one-step form.")
       (notes-statement "If {x_n} is non-decreasing and bounded above then lim_n x_n = sup_n x_n; if non-increasing and bounded below then lim_n x_n = inf_n x_n."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.9")
       (section "1.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Closed sets and limits of sequences")
       (names closed-iff-sequentially-closed seq-closed-implies-closed closed-limit-in)
       (home-elsewhere closed-iff-sequentially-closed seq-closed-implies-closed closed-limit-in)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "A subset PTS(s) is a hypothesis of the iff; sequences typed f in FUN(NN, A).")
       (notes-statement "A set A in a metric space X is closed if and only if every sequence of elements of A which converges, converges to an element of A."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.11")
       (section "1.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Characterisations of compactness")
       (names compact-iff-fip compact-iff-cluster-point compact-iff-tb-complete)
       (home-elsewhere compact-iff-fip compact-iff-cluster-point compact-iff-tb-complete)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "All four conditions are present and each equivalence is proven, but as three separate IFF theorems against (1) rather than one four-way equivalence. In (2) the library's HAS-FIP carries two inhabitedness guards the notes do not: the family c must be non-empty (forsome a0. a0 in c), and only NON-EMPTY finite subfamilies are required to have a non-empty intersection. (3) is stated with CLUSTER-POINT defined by: for every eps > 0 and every m in NN there is n >= m with dist(f(n), x) < eps -- the notes define a cluster point by the set {n : x_n in B(y,r)} being infinite.")
       (notes-statement "For a metric space X the following are equivalent: (1) X is compact; (2) X satisfies the finite intersection property for closed sets; (3) every sequence in X has a cluster point; (4) X is totally bounded and complete."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.12")
       (section "1.4" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Product of numbers in polar form")
       (names cc-cos-add cc-sin-add cc-exp-add cc-polar-form)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The identity z * w = r s (cos(theta + tau) + i sin(theta + tau)) is not stated; cc-cos-add, cc-sin-add (and cc-exp-add with cc-polar-form) give it in a few lines.")
       (notes-statement "If z = r(cos theta + i sin theta) and w = s(cos tau + i sin tau) then z w = r s (cos(theta + tau) + i sin(theta + tau))."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.14")
       (section "1.4" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Positive orientation of a triangular path by angles")
       (names positively-oriented-iff)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "ANGLE(a, b) is pi + Im of the strip logarithm of -(a conj b), the argument of a conj b in [0, 2 pi). Guards: a, b, c distinct, and the interior of conv{a, b, c} nonempty.")
       (notes-statement "The triangular path [a, b, c] is positively oriented iff for some u in the interior of conv{a, b, c} the angles angle(b - u, a - u), angle(c - u, b - u), angle(a - u, c - u) are all < pi."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.15")
       (section "1.5" prefilled)
       (kind theorem)
       (title "C is an algebraically closed field")
       (names cc-algebraically-closed fundamental-theorem-of-algebra cc-polynomial-lower-growth)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The polynomial is a coefficient function in FUN(NN, CC) and its value a partial sum CC-SERIES-PARTIAL-SUM, there being no evaluation map from POLY(CC) to FUN(CC, CC). The route is Cauchy's estimate applied to 1/P (fundamental-theorem-of-algebra, 2026-10-04), not the notes' minimum-modulus argument, which needs n-th roots of complex numbers. The notes' growth estimate (6)-(7) is cc-polynomial-lower-growth, with a non-strict threshold.")
       (notes-statement "C is an algebraically closed field: every polynomial equation a_n z^n + ... + a_0 = 0 with n >= 1 and a_n /= 0 has a solution in C."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.16")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Convergence in the extended plane")
       (names cc-hat-converges-iff cc-hat-converges-inf-iff)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "C-hat is CC-HAT = CC u {CC-INF}, CC-INF a fresh constant with the two PRIMITIVE facts cc-inf-in-set and cc-inf-not-in-cc (the user's decision, 2026-10-19). Convergence is TOP-CONVERGES-TO in CC-HAT-TOP, whose open sets are the notes' definition; that CC-HAT-TOP is a topology is not proven. 'abs(x_n) -> +infinity' is stated as: for every real M, M <= abs(f(k)) for all large k.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {x_n} in C: it converges to x in C in the topology of C-hat iff it converges to x in the Euclidean metric; it converges to infinity iff |x_n| -> +infinity."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.18")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Linear maps on C^2 and linear fractional transformations")
       (names mat-lft-in-lfts mat-lft-hom mat-lft-onto mat-lft-kernel lft-homog lft-compose)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The projective plane P is not formed: 'S factors through P' is read through the identification (9) of P with C-hat (lft-homog: L_S(phi(u, v)) = phi(S(u, v))). The homomorphism is stated as L_(ST) = L_S o L_T for invertible complex 2x2 matrices, with surjectivity onto LFTS and kernel the matrices r I. The kernel is found by evaluating at infinity, 0 and 1, not by comparing polynomials.")
       (notes-statement "An invertible complex linear map S on C^2 with matrix [[a,b],[c,d]] factors through a transformation L_S on the projective plane P; under the identification of P with C-hat, S |-> L_S is a surjective homomorphism from the group of invertible complex linear maps onto the set of linear fractional transformations, with kernel the scalar multiples of the identity."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.19")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The linear fractional transformations form a group")
       (names lft-group-is-group)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "IS-GROUP(LFT-GROUP), LFT-GROUP being LFTS with composition, the identity of CC-HAT and INVERSE-BIJ. The inverse is computed explicitly (the transformation of the adjugate), not obtained as the image of a group under a homomorphism.")
       (notes-statement "The set of linear fractional transformations is a group."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.20")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "SL(2,C) onto the linear fractional transformations")
       (names sl2-lft-hom sl2-lft-onto sl2-lft-kernel sl2-matmul-closed)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Determinant 1 is DET(R, 2, S) = 1 over the ring view of CC-NORMED-FIELD; that these matrices form a group is stated as closure under products (sl2-matmul-closed). The square root of d is taken through exp on its strip (cc-sqrt-exists).")
       (notes-statement "S |-> L_S is a surjective homomorphism from the group of linear maps on C^2 with determinant 1 onto the set of linear fractional transformations, with kernel {-I, I}."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.21")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The Cayley transform")
       (names cayley-real-circle cayley-upper-disc)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "D is BALL(CC-MS, 0, 1) and CAYLEY is LFT(1, -i, 1, i). The disc statement is proved from the identity |w + i|^2 = |w - i|^2 + 4 Im w, not by connectedness of H+.")
       (notes-statement "The linear fractional transformation C z = (z - i)/(z + i) maps R bijectively onto T minus {1}, and maps the upper half plane H+ bijectively onto the open unit disc D."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.22")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The Hopf map is continuous and onto S^2")
       (names hopf-maps-sphere hopf-onto-sphere hopf-map-continuous-product hopf-map-continuous hopf-norm-identity)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "C^2 is PRODUCT2-MS(CC-MS, CC-MS) and C x R is PRODUCT2-MS(CC-MS, RR-MS), the binary product metric spaces with the max metric (structure-library/product2-vocab); S^3 = CC2-SPHERE, S^2 = UNIT-SPHERE-CR; pi is the map w |-> HOPF-MAP(w) on CARTESIAN(CC, CC). Continuity is IS-CONTINUOUS(C^2, C x R, pi) (hopf-map-continuous-product), assembled from the general form hopf-map-continuous (for u1, u2 : S -> C continuous at x, the two coordinates of z |-> pi(u1(z), u2(z)) are continuous at x) and the pairing property of the product. Onto by an explicit preimage [k (1 + r), k conj(z)], k = 1/sqrt(2 (1 + r)).")
       (notes-statement "pi(u1, u2) = (2 u1 conj(u2), |u1|^2 - |u2|^2) is a continuous mapping C^2 -> C x R, and maps the sphere S^3 of C^2 onto the unit sphere S^2 of R^2 x R."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "1.24")
       (section "1.6" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The Hopf fibration factors through a bijection S^2 -> P")
       (names hopf-factors-through-bijection hopf-fibre-equiv hopf-fibre-iff)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "P is read through phi = HOMOG-POINT as C u {oo} (CA-LFT). The bijection is HOPF-QUOTIENT-MAP, [z, r] |-> phi(z, 1 - r), with g(pi(u)) = phi(u1, u2) on S^3. The fibres (pi(u) = pi(v) iff u ~ v on S^3) are proved with the witness r = <v, u> of (13), not through (18).")
       (notes-statement "The mapping pi factors through a bijection S^2 -> P."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.2")
       (section "2.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Differentiable implies continuous")
       (names diff-on-implies-continuous)
       (home-elsewhere diff-on-implies-continuous)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for an arbitrary normed field K rather than for CC (more general; the CC instance is got by taking K = CC-NORMED-FIELD, but no CC-specialised corollary is installed). The conclusion is continuity at a in the SUBSPACE metric on U -- is-continuous-at(subspace-ms(nf-metric-space(k), u), nf-metric-space(k), f, a) -- not continuity of a map defined near a in C; the two agree because IS-DIFF-ON forces U open, but the library does not say so.")
       (notes-statement "If f : U -> C is differentiable at a in U (U open) then f is continuous at a."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.3")
       (section "2.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Sum and product rules")
       (names diff-on-sum diff-on-product holomorphic-sum holomorphic-product)
       (home-elsewhere diff-on-sum diff-on-product)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Two separate developments, neither of which is exactly the notes' statement. diff-on-sum / diff-on-product give the derivative at a, but only for the LITERAL function VNB-LAMBDA x in U. (add K)(f(x), g(x)) (resp. the product); a pointwise-equal representative of f + g or f . g has to be transported by diff-on-transfer-ptwise-eq. holomorphic-sum / holomorphic-product do take an arbitrary sm in FUN(U, CC) that agrees pointwise, but they conclude only HOLOMORPHIC-ON(U, sm) and say nothing about the value of the derivative at a. Both diff-on laws are stated for a general normed field K.")
       (notes-statement "If f, g are differentiable at a then f + g and f . g are differentiable at a, with [f+g]'(a) = f'(a) + g'(a) and [f.g]'(a) = f'(a) g(a) + f(a) g'(a)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.3.3.1")
       (section "2.1" prefilled-inferred)
       (kind definition)
       (title "The principal branch of the logarithm")
       (names cc-log-spec cc-exp-log cc-log-exp cc-log-char cc-log-unique cc-log-real cc-log-one cc-log-polar cc-log-scale cc-log-in-slit)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "DEFINED 2026-09-27 (batch 38): CC-LOG(w) is the IOTA of the z with -pi < Im z < pi and exp z = w, for w in the slit plane {u in cc : not(Im u = 0 and Re u <= 0)} (structure-library/analytic-log.scm); existence is cc-exp-open-strip-onto (batch 34), uniqueness cc-log-unique, the characterisation cc-log-spec by iota-d. Laws: exp(log w) = w, log(exp z) = z on the strip, log = LOG on the positive reals (through the bridge cc-exp-real-is-r-exp), log 1 = 0, the polar form (40) log(r exp(i th)) = LOG r + i th, log(s w) = LOG s + log w for real s > 0.")
       (notes-statement "log is the function on C minus (-inf, 0] with values in {z : -pi < Im z < pi} such that exp(log z) = z; it exists and is unique by Proposition 2.19."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.3.3.1-hol")
       (section "2.1" prefilled-inferred)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "log is holomorphic on C minus (-inf, 0]")
       (names cc-log-holomorphic cc-log-deriv cc-log-holomorphic-at cc-log-im-near-one cc-log-rotate cc-log-rotate-neg cc-log-mul cc-log-right-half cc-pi-gt-three cc-cos-pos-half cc-log-holomorphic-near-one cc-log-holomorphic-scale)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-27 (batch 38-B), with the derivative log'(w) = recip(w) (cc-log-deriv). THE ROUTE DIFFERS FROM THE NOTES: no sector cover and no numerical bound on pi is needed -- at every point a of the slit plane, log x = log a + log(x / a) on the ball B(a, |a| r) with r = (pi - |Im log a|) / (2 (1 + pi)), where log(x / a) is the power series near 1 (cc-log-holomorphic-at, cc-log-im-near-one). The notes' rotation laws log(i w) = log w + i pi/2 (cc-log-rotate, from cos > 0 on (-pi/2, pi/2), cc-cos-pos-half) and pi > 3 (cc-pi-gt-three, from cos(3/2) > 0 by six terms of the series) are proven as well. Earlier: PARTIAL 2026-09-27 (batch 38): holomorphic on B(1, pi/(1+pi)) (as the power series log0) and on every scaled ball B(s, s pi/(1+pi)), s > 0, by log(s w) = LOG s + log w. The rotation step log(i w) = log w + i pi/2 and the cover of the slit plane need cos > 0 on (-pi/2, pi/2) and pi > 3, which the tree lacked: batch 38-B.")
       (notes-statement "log is holomorphic on C minus (-inf, 0] (shown directly: as a power series near 1, then by scaling to the right half-plane, then by rotation)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.4")
       (section "2.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Chain Rule")
       (names diff-on-chain)
       (home-elsewhere diff-on-chain)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The library adds hypotheses the notes leave implicit: both U and the target set S must be open (carried by IS-DIFF-ON), and forall y in U. f(y) in S is an explicit antecedent (the notes only require g to be differentiable at f(a)). The composite is the literal VNB-LAMBDA x in U. g(f(x)), so a pointwise-equal representative needs diff-on-transfer-ptwise-eq; the derivative is written (mul K)(m, l) in the normed field K. Stated for a general K, not specialised to CC.")
       (notes-statement "If f is differentiable at a and g is differentiable at f(a), then g o f is differentiable at a and (g o f)'(a) = g'(f(a)) f'(a)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.5")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Root Test")
       (names root-test-converges root-test-diverges)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 25): PROVEN as the notes state it, both halves, for a non-negative real series, with the lim sup taken in [0, +inf] (ELIMSUP; every lim sup the notes take is of a non-negative sequence), theorem-library/limsup-tests.scm. EARLIER NOTE: No root test in any form. The library has no lim sup for real sequences: ELIMSUP / ELIMINF exist only for sequences into RR-POS-STAR = [0, +inf] and carry no theory beyond the definition of ECONVERGES-TO. The nearest convergence criteria for a non-negative real series are comparison-test (termwise domination by a convergent series) and geometric-series-converges; the divergence half has no analogue at all.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {theta_k} of non-negative reals with rho = lim sup_k theta_k^(1/k): the series sum_k theta_k converges if rho < 1 and diverges if rho > 1."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.7")
       (section "2.2" prefilled-inferred)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Radius of convergence by the root test")
       (names cps-radius-root)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-11-09 (THEOREMS (cps-radius-root states the three root-test cases); orig names cps-radius-formula absent from THEOREMS); see scratchpad/triage/THEOREMS.md (cps-radius-root states the three root-test cases); orig names cps-radius-formula absent from THEOREMS.md.")
       (notes-statement "The radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k is 1 / lim sup_k |a_k|^(1/k)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.8")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Normal convergence inside the radius")
       (names cps-normally-convergent cps-abs-conv-closed-ball cps-converges-closed-ball)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-11-09 (THEOREMS (cps-normally-convergent); orig names cps-normally-convergent-inside cps-converges-inside absent from THEOREMS); see scratchpad/triage/THEOREMS.md (cps-normally-convergent); orig names cps-normally-convergent-inside cps-converges-inside absent from THEOREMS.md.")
       (notes-statement "If R is the radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k then for every r < R the series is normally convergent on the closed ball B-bar(0,r); in particular it converges absolutely there and the convergence is uniform."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.9")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Polynomially bounded factors do not shrink the radius")
       (names cps-radius-factor)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-11-09 (THEOREMS (cps-radius-factor states the polynomial-bound comparison); orig name cps-radius-poly-factor absent from THEOREMS); see scratchpad/triage/THEOREMS.md (cps-radius-factor states the polynomial-bound comparison); orig name cps-radius-poly-factor absent from THEOREMS.md.")
       (notes-statement "If |b_k| <= C k^m for some C, m, then the radius of convergence of sum_k a_k z^k is at most that of sum_k a_k b_k z^k."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.10")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A power series is holomorphic and differentiates termwise")
       (names cps-holomorphic-ball cps-radius-derived cps-diff-on-ball)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 26-A, theorem-library/cc-power-series-deriv.scm): PROVEN with one convention -- the library has no ball of radius +inf, so the statement is made on B(0, r) for every real 0 < r < R (which covers B(0, R) for finite R and CC for R = +inf): f is holomorphic on B(0, r), the derived series sum (k+1) a_(k+1) z^k has the same radius, and f'(u) is its sum. The proof avoids the notes' double sum and lim n^(1/n) = 1 (absent): a one-step recurrence gives the second-order estimate, a radius-comparison lemma and Lemma 2.9 with b_k = k give the radius. EARLIER NOTE: Nothing near, in either the complex or the real case. The library's power series are real and pointwise (PS-CONVERGES-AT); it has no theorem that the sum function of a power series is differentiable, no termwise-differentiated series, and no statement that the two series share a radius. HOLOMORPHIC-ON is available (holomorphic-basics.scm) but the only holomorphic functions exhibited are constants, the identity, powers, sums and products (holomorphic-const, holomorphic-identity, holomorphic-power, holomorphic-sum, holomorphic-product).")
       (notes-statement "If sum_k a_k z^k has radius of convergence R then f(z) = sum_k a_k z^k is defined and holomorphic on B(0,R); the series g(z) = sum_{n>=1} n a_n z^(n-1) also has radius R, and f'(z) = g(z)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.12")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Principle of analytic continuation")
       (names analytic-continuation holomorphic-identity-principle holomorphic-zeros-local holomorphic-zero-coefficients)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-14 as the notes state it: u open and IS-CONNECTED(CC-MS, u), f in FUN(u, CC) analytic at every point of u, and a point a of u with ZEROS-ACCUMULATE-AT(u, f, a) (every ball about a contains a zero of f in u other than a) give f(z) = 0 for every z in u. The route differs from the notes: connectedness (connected-locally-constant) replaces the largest initial subpath of a polygonal path, and the local step shows that every Taylor coefficient at a vanishes instead of factoring f = (z - a)^n g with g(a) /= 0. holomorphic-identity-principle is the same statement for f holomorphic on u.")
       (notes-statement "Let U be a connected open set. If f is analytic in U and the set of zeros of f in U has an accumulation point in U, then f is identically 0."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.13")
       (section "2.2" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Finitely many zeros in a closed ball")
       (names analytic-zeros-finite-in-ball compact-cover-by-balls)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-14: with u and f as in 2.12, f(e) /= 0 for some e in u (not identically 0), and CLOSED-BALL(CC-MS, c, r) a subset of u, the set of zeros of f in the ball has CARD in NN. The proof uses a finite subcover of the compact ball (compact-cover-by-balls) instead of an injective sequence of zeros.")
       (notes-statement "Let U be a connected open set. If f is analytic in U and not identically 0, then within any closed ball B contained in U, f has only finitely many zeros."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.14")
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Taylor coefficients of an analytic function")
       (names taylor-coefficients-at taylor-coefficients taylor-coefficient-formula cps-translate-nth-deriv analytic-holomorphic-ball cps-taylor-coefficients)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-27 (batch 38) AT A CENTRE: IS-ANALYTIC-AT(f, U, a) is DEFINED (structure-library/analytic-log.scm, the notes' 2.11: U open, f in fun(U, cc), a power series of positive radius on a ball B(a, r) inside U), the power-series layer is translated from 0 to a (cps-translate-*), and taylor-coefficients-at states f^(k)(a) = k! a_k with f^(k) the k-th NTH-DERIV-ON of f restricted to B(a, r). Earlier: UPDATED 2026-09-26 (batch 36): NTH-DERIV-ON(K, U, f, k) is DEFINED by NN-recursion (structure-library/nth-deriv.scm) and 2.14 is stated LITERALLY -- taylor-coefficients: the k-th derivative of the sum function at 0 is k! a_k, for the power series at 0 on every ball B(0, r), r < radius; taylor-coefficient-formula is the coefficient form. Still partial for one reason only: the notes' 'f analytic at a' (no IS-ANALYTIC predicate, no translation to a centre a). Earlier: Proven 2026-09-24 (batch 27-A) for a function GIVEN as a power series at 0 (the notes' proof reduces to this case by translation; the tree has no IS-ANALYTIC predicate, so the hypothesis 'f analytic at a' is not stated). The tree has no iterated derivative over CC: f^(k) is read as F_k, the sum function of the k-th derived series, whose coefficients (n+k)!/n! a_(n+k) are given by their values. For every real 0 < r < R: F_k is holomorphic on B(0, r), DERIV-ON(F_k) = F_(k+1) on the ball, and F_k(0) = k! a_k; hence a_k = F_k(0)/k! (the second name).")
       (notes-statement "If f is analytic at a then the formal power series sum_k f^(k)(a) z^k has positive radius of convergence and f(z) = sum_k f^(k)(a)/k! (z-a)^k for z near a."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.15")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Ratio Test")
       (names ratio-test-converges-limsup ratio-test-diverges-liminf ratio-test-converges)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-24 (batch 25): the first half PROVEN as stated (lim sup of the ratios < 1). THE SECOND HALF IS FALSE AS PRINTED: lim sup theta_(k+1)/theta_k > 1 does not imply divergence (theta_(2j) = 4^-j, theta_(2j+1) = 2 * 4^-j has ratios alternating 2 and 1/8, lim sup 2, and sums to 4); the library states and proves the divergence half with lim INF > 1. The older asserted ratio-test-converges (a real power series with a LIMIT of ratios) follows from the new one with two short bridges and remains asserted for now. EARLIER NOTE: A PSS entry (warrant: informal), not proven. The library's ratio test is not the notes' statement about a bare series of positive reals: it is about a REAL POWER SERIES, coef in FUN(NN, RR) with every coef(n) non-zero, and the ratio hypothesis is PS-RATIO-LIMIT(coef, l) -- the ratios |coef(n+1)/coef(n)| CONVERGE to l, a limit, not a lim sup -- with l in RR, 0 <= l; the conclusion is PS-CONVERGES-AT(coef, x) for every real x with |x| l < 1. The divergence half (lim sup of the ratios > 1 implies divergence) is absent.")
       (notes-statement "For a sequence {theta_k} of positive reals: if lim sup_k theta_(k+1)/theta_k < 1 then sum_k theta_k converges; if lim sup_k theta_(k+1)/theta_k > 1 it diverges."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.16")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The exponential series has infinite radius")
       (names cc-exp-radius cps-radius-pos-inf cc-cos-radius cc-sin-radius)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 31-A, the user's decision: exp, cos, sin DEFINED by their power series, structure-library/cc-elementary.scm): PROVEN, cc-exp-radius (cps-radius of the coefficients 1/k! is pos-inf, by the notes' ratio test), with the same for the cos and sin series. Nothing near: no radius of convergence, and the series sum_k z^k / k! is nowhere formed. FACTORIAL exists (factorial-real-pos, recip-factorial-in-rr) and is used inside the Taylor polynomial, but there is no theorem about the convergence of the exponential series, real or complex.")
       (notes-statement "The radius of convergence of sum_k z^k / k! is infinite."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.17")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "exp is a continuous homomorphism into C*")
       (names cc-exp-add cc-exp-zero cc-exp-neg cc-exp-nonzero cc-exp-maps-to-nonzero cc-exp-continuous cc-exp-deriv cc-exp-holomorphic)
       (home-elsewhere cc-exp-add)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 31-A): PROVEN for the COMPLEX exponential defined by its series (theorem-library/cc-exp.scm): exp(z + w) = exp z exp w (cc-exp-add, by the zero-derivative route along a segment: exp(z) exp(w - z) is constant, no Cauchy product needed), exp 0 = 1, exp(-z) = 1/exp z, exp z /= 0, continuity and exp' = exp on CC. The tree has no group-homomorphism predicate, so 2.17 is these equations. The real exponential R-EXP (inverse of the integral LOG) is a separate object; the bridge 'exp restricted to RR is R-EXP' is not yet stated. There is no complex exponential in the library. R-EXP is the REAL exponential, and it is not defined by the power series: LOG is defined as the oriented integral of 1/x from 1 (log-unfold) and R-EXP is its inverse, so the identification of R-EXP with the restriction of the notes' exp is not a library fact. What is proven is the real restriction of the statement: r-exp-add (exp(u+v) = exp(u) exp(v) for real u, v), r-exp-pos (0 < exp(y), i.e. the values land in the multiplicative group of the positive reals) and r-exp-continuous-at (continuity at each real point, in RR-MS). Nothing about C or C* = C minus {0}.")
       (notes-statement "z |-> exp z is a continuous homomorphism from the additive group of C into the multiplicative group C* = C minus {0}."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.18")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The kernel of exp is 2 pi i Z")
       (names cc-exp-one-iff pi-spec cc-exp-kernel-generator cc-exp-kernel-generator-unique cc-exp-two-pi-i cc-exp-i-pi)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 31-B, the user's decision on PI): PROVEN, cc-exp-one-iff: exp z = 1 iff z = k (2 pi i) for some integer k. PI is DEFINED (structure-library/cc-pi.scm) as the positive real p with kernel(exp) = ZZ (2 p i), i.e. the generator of the kernel in the upper half plane divided by 2i; existence (the kernel is a subgroup of i RR, discrete, non-trivial since cos has a zero in (0,2), hence cyclic) and uniqueness are proven before the IOTA is formed. The notes use pi without defining it. Nothing near: no complex exponential, no pi (the symbol pi occurs in THEOREMS.md only as a bound variable in pigeonhole-infinite), no trigonometric functions. The real fragment of the statement -- exp t = 1 for real t only when t = 0 -- follows from r-exp-strictly-increasing and r-exp-zero but is not itself installed.")
       (notes-statement "z |-> exp z takes the value 1 precisely when z is an integer multiple of 2 pi i."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.19")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "exp is a bijection on a horizontal strip")
       (names cc-exp-bijection-strip cc-exp-bijection-open-strip cc-exp-surjective-strip cc-exp-injective-strip cc-exp-open-strip-onto cc-exp-open-strip-avoids-negatives cc-polar-form cc-exp-real-is-r-exp)
       (home-elsewhere cc-polar-form)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 34) as the notes state it: cc-exp-bijection-strip says the lambda z in S. exp z is in bijection(S, cc minus {0}) for S the SEP {-pi <= Im < pi}; cc-exp-bijection-open-strip the same for the open strip onto cc minus (-inf, 0]. Surjectivity goes through the polar form (cc-polar-form, cc-circle-arg by the IVT on cos) and the bridge cc-exp-real-is-r-exp (exp restricted to RR is the real exponential R-EXP, via the derivative of log(exp t) - t). Earlier: UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 31-B): the INJECTIVITY half is PROVEN for the complex exponential on the strip -pi <= Im z < pi (cc-exp-injective-strip, by Lemma 2.18); surjectivity onto CC minus 0 and the open-strip statement wait on the polar form (exp unbounded on RR, IVT for the modulus and the argument). Only the real restriction, and under the library's own definition of R-EXP as the inverse of the integral LOG rather than as a power series. r-exp-log and log-r-exp say that R-EXP and LOG are mutually inverse between RR and the positive reals, log-surjective and r-exp-pos give surjectivity onto (0, +inf), and log-injective-pos / r-exp-strictly-increasing give injectivity. Nothing about the strips S = {-pi <= Im z < pi} or S' = {-pi < Im z < pi}, about C minus {0} or about C minus (-inf, 0]; there is no pi and no complex exponential to state them with.")
       (notes-statement "exp is a bijection from S = {z : -pi <= Im z < pi} onto C minus {0}, and a bijection from S' = {z : -pi < Im z < pi} onto C minus (-inf, 0]."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.20")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The logarithm series has radius 1")
       (names cps-log-radius-one cps-log-series cps-log-radius cps-log-radius-le-one cc-series-terms-small cc-geometric-alternating)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-27 (batches 38 and 38-B): cps-radius of the series is exactly 1 (cps-log-radius-one; the upper half through the new general lemma cc-series-terms-small, the terms of a convergent complex series tend to 0, and Bernoulli), and the derivative is recip(1 + z) on every B(0, r), r < 1. Earlier: PARTIAL 2026-09-27 (batch 38): the series is formed (as a lambda inside the theorems), its radius is at least 1 (cps-log-radius) and its derivative is recip(1 + z) on every B(0, r), r < 1 (cps-log-series, by 2.10 termwise and the alternating geometric series cc-geometric-alternating). The other half, radius at most 1, is batch 38-B's item (4). Earlier: Nothing near: no radius of convergence, and the series sum_k (-1)^(k-1) z^k / k is nowhere formed. The library's derivative of the logarithm is log-deriv (is-diff-at of x |-> log(x) at c > 0 with value recip-star(c)), proved from the integral definition, not from a series.")
       (notes-statement "f(z) = sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) z^k / k has radius of convergence 1, and f'(z) = (1 + z)^(-1)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.21")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The logarithm series agrees with ln on (0,2)")
       (names log0-is-ln log0-exp log0-is-log-on-ball cc-log-holomorphic-near-one)
       (home-elsewhere cc-log-holomorphic-near-one)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-27 (batch 38) in the notes' wording: log0-is-ln says the series (39) at a real t in (0, 2) equals LOG(t), by the notes' argument (equal derivatives, equal value at 1). Also exp(log0 z) = z on every B(1, r), r < 1 (log0-exp), and log0 IS the principal logarithm CC-LOG on B(1, pi/(1+pi)) (log0-is-log-on-ball: the radius where |log0 z| < pi keeps the value in the strip; the full B(1, 1) waits for the identity theorem 3.35). Earlier: Nothing near: the power series log_0 z = sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) (z-1)^k / k does not exist in the library. LOG itself is present and is exactly the notes' ln (log-unfold: LOG(x) = the oriented integral of 1/z from 1 to x, with log-one, log-mul, log-deriv, log-strictly-increasing proven), so only the series and its identification with LOG are missing.")
       (notes-statement "The function log_0 defined by the series sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k-1) (z-1)^k / k coincides on the open interval (0,2) with the natural logarithm ln on the positive reals."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "2.22")
       (section "2.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The series of log(1 - z) and log(1 + z)")
       (names cc-log-one-minus-disc cc-log-one-plus-disc log0-is-log-on-unit-ball cc-log-of-deriv-on-ball cc-log-one-minus cc-log-one-plus)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-26 on the full disc |z| < 1. The notes' differentiation argument: log' = 1/w on the slit plane (cc-log-deriv); so log - log0 has derivative 0 on every B(1, r), r < 1, and vanishes at 1 (cc-log-of-deriv-on-ball); hence log = log0 on B(1, 1) (log0-is-log-on-unit-ball). No identity theorem or connectedness lemma is needed. Absolute convergence: cps-abs-conv-closed-ball with cps-log-radius.")
       (notes-statement "log(1 - z) = - sum_{k>=1} z^k / k and log(1 + z) = sum_{k>=1} (-1)^(k+1) z^k / k, both absolutely convergent for |z| < 1."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.1")
       (section "3.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Path additivity of the line integral")
       (names line-int-concat line-int-juxta juxta-is-road regulated-on-glue tri-int-is-line-int line-int-length-bound line-int-adjacent road-restrict)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 37): the juxtaposition JUXTA(gamma, rho, a, b, c) of roads on adjacent intervals (Dieudonne 9.6; structure-library/road-juxtaposition.scm) is a road (juxta-is-road, with the new regulated-on-glue) and the integral adds (line-int-juxta); the notes' (49) for gamma on [a, b] and rho on [c, d] with gamma(b) = rho(c) is line-int-concat, rho shifted to [b, b + (d - c)]. Also the triangular road TRI-ROAD on [0, 3] with tri-int-is-line-int, and ROAD-LENGTH (the integral of |gamma'|) with the length estimate (53), line-int-length-bound. Earlier: UPDATED 2026-09-23 (batch 24): the SPLIT form is proven -- line-int-adjacent: for a road gamma on [a, b], c in (a, b) and f continuous on a set containing the trace, the integral along gamma is the sum of the integrals along the restrictions to [a, c] and [c, b] (road-restrict: the restriction of a road is a road). The CONCATENATION form of the notes (gamma || rho, two roads glued at a point) is not stated: it needs the glued road, which is primitive-glue (batch 22) applied to each coordinate. Existence of the integral for a road and a continuous f is line-int-exists (batch 23). EARLIER NOTE: Not in the library. The DEFINITIONS exist since 2026-09-21, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b). Proven since: the integral pw-int is well defined (two piecewise primitives have the same endpoint difference), linear, additive over adjacent intervals and monotone; cc-int is linear over CC and additive; the notes' estimate (45) (cc-int-abs-bound); the trace of a path is compact (path-trace-compact); line-int of 1 along a road is gamma(b) - gamma(a); the opposite road and Dieudonne (9.6.1). Existence of a primitive of a piecewise continuous function (Dieudonne 8.7.2) is NOT proven, so the primitives of an integrand's coordinates are hypotheses wherever a value of an integral is asserted. Juxtaposition of two roads (Dieudonne 9.6.2) is not defined yet.")
       (notes-statement "If gamma, rho are paths such that the concatenation gamma || rho is defined, then the integral of f over gamma || rho is the integral over gamma plus the integral over rho."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.2")
       (section "3.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Fundamental theorem for line integrals")
       (names line-int-of-derivative holomorphic-chain)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-09-23 (line-int-of-derivative): f holomorphic on the open set U, df in fun(U, CC) its derivative (is-diff-on at every point of U), gamma a road ON [a, b] with derivative dgamma and trace inside U; then line-int(df, gamma, dgamma, a, b) = f(gamma(b)) - f(gamma(a)). The derivative is a PARAMETER df rather than the notes' f', because line-int takes a function as integrand. No primitive of the integrand is assumed: f o gamma is the primitive (holomorphic-chain, the chain rule of a holomorphic f with a road, coordinatewise). Roads are the tree's is-road with a finite exceptional set (restatement over Dieudonne's regulated primitives pending the user's decision).")
       (notes-statement "If f is holomorphic in the open set U and gamma : [alpha,beta] -> U is a path, then the integral of f' along gamma is f(gamma(beta)) - f(gamma(alpha))."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.3")
       (section "3.1" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Polynomials integrate to zero over a closed path")
       (names poly-fn-closed-road-integral cc-ps-antiderivative line-int-closed-road-of-derivative affine-closed-road-integral)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Proven 2026-10-28 (CAL-INT) as the notes state it, for a polynomial function on CC in POLY-2's sense (IS-POLY-FN-ON(CC, CC, f)) and a closed road (pgam(b) = pgam(a)). The antiderivative is cc-ps-antiderivative. Paths are the tree's roads.")
       (notes-statement "If f is a polynomial function and gamma is a closed path, then the integral of f along gamma is 0."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.4")
       (section "3.1" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "Splitting a segment integral at an interior point")
       (names segment-int-split segment-int-abs-bound segment-int-reverse line-int-affine-reparam line-int-adjacent)
       (home-elsewhere line-int-adjacent)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UPDATED 2026-09-25 (batch 29-A): PROVEN for the segment itself (theorem-library/line-int-reparam.scm): segment-int-split splits the integral along the segment z0 + s w, s in [0,1], at the interior point z0 + t w (0 < t < 1) into the integrals along the two sub-segments, through the affine reparametrisation of the line integral, the notes' (48) (line-int-affine-reparam). The endpoint cases and the restatement with the segment functoid SEG-INT are batch 30. UPDATED 2026-09-23 (batch 24): proven for ANY road, line-int-adjacent (split at an interior point c of [a, b]); the segment of the notes is the special case where gamma is a straight segment (segment-is-road), and the restriction of a segment is not restated as a segment. EARLIER NOTE: Not in the library. The DEFINITIONS exist since 2026-09-21, for functions ON [a, b] (structure-library/path-integral.scm): is-path, is-road(gamma, dgamma, a, b) with the derivative dgamma an explicit argument, trace, cc-int (the notes' equation (44)) and line-int(f, gamma, dgamma, a, b). Proven since: the integral pw-int is well defined (two piecewise primitives have the same endpoint difference), linear, additive over adjacent intervals and monotone; cc-int is linear over CC and additive; the notes' estimate (45) (cc-int-abs-bound); the trace of a path is compact (path-trace-compact); line-int of 1 along a road is gamma(b) - gamma(a); the opposite road and Dieudonne (9.6.1). Existence of a primitive of a piecewise continuous function (Dieudonne 8.7.2) is NOT proven, so the primitives of an integrand's coordinates are hypotheses wherever a value of an integral is asserted.")
       (notes-statement "If c is an element of the segment <a,b>, then the integral of f along <a,b> is the integral along <a,c> plus the integral along <c,b>."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.5")
       (section "3.2" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The barycentric-subdivision sum")
       (names tri-int-subdivision seg-int-split-at segment-int-reverse)
       (home-elsewhere segment-int-reverse)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-25 (batch 30, theorem-library/goursat.scm). The triangle integral TRI-INT(f, a, b, c) is DEFINED as the sum of the three segment integrals (the user's decision, docs/goursat-definitions-2026-09-25.md), so the triangular path is not a road object; the subdivision S(triangle) is written out as the four vertex triples with CC-MID. Hypothesis: f continuous on a set containing CONV3(a, b, c).")
       (notes-statement "If f is continuous on the convex hull of a triangular path, the integral around the triangle is the sum of the integrals around the four triangles of its barycentric subdivision."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.6")
       (section "3.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Cauchy's theorem for triangles (Goursat)")
       (names goursat-triangle-except goursat-vertex tri-int-fan tri-int-vertex-split conv3-avoids-vertex collinear-cases tri-int-collinear tri-int-restrict goursat-triangle)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 35) WITH THE EXCEPTIONAL POINT: the exceptional-point hypothesis is the notes' (f continuous on the open set u, x in u, f holomorphic on u minus {x}), spelled with restrict(f, difference(u, singleton(x))) because HOLOMORPHIC-ON types its function on exactly its set. Proof: x off the hull, Goursat on u minus {x}; x in the hull, the fan through x and the vertex case three times; the vertex case is Rudin 10.13 (shrink the triangle towards the vertex; the two remaining triangles avoid x by affine independence; the collinear triangle through the three placements). Earlier: PROVEN 2026-09-25 (batch 30) for f HOLOMORPHIC ON ALL OF U: holomorphic-on(U, f), a, b, c in U, CONV3(a, b, c) subset U implies TRI-INT(f, a, b, c) = 0 (goursat-triangle). The notes' hypothesis 'continuous on U and holomorphic on U minus a point x' is the strengthening of a later batch, needed by the Cauchy integral formula. The proof departs from the notes at the common point of the nested triangles: no Cantor intersection and no compactness; the chosen sub-triangles are driven on the standard triangle and the vertex sequence telescopes (cc-telescoping-limit-bound), its limit lying in CONV3 by definition. No diameter and no length are defined: the estimates are per segment with the perimeter as a term.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose f is continuous on an open set U and holomorphic on U minus a point x. If a, b, c in U are such that conv{a, b, c} is contained in U, then the integral of f around the triangle [a, b, c] is 0."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.7")
       (section "3.2" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Segment integrals in a convex set")
       (names cauchy-convex-segments-except cauchy-convex-segments)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 35) with the exceptional point; the exceptional-point hypothesis is the notes' (f continuous on the open set u, x in u, f holomorphic on u minus {x}), spelled with restrict(f, difference(u, singleton(x))) because HOLOMORPHIC-ON types its function on exactly its set. Earlier: PROVEN 2026-09-25 (batch 30) for f holomorphic on the whole convex open set U (is-convex(U), holomorphic-on(U, f), a, b, c in U implies SEG-INT(f, a, c) = SEG-INT(f, a, b) + SEG-INT(f, b, c)); the exceptional point x as for 3.6.")
       (notes-statement "If f is continuous on an open convex set U and holomorphic on U minus a point x, then for a, b, c in U the integral along <a, c> is the integral along <a, b> plus the integral along <b, c>."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.8")
       (section "3.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A primitive on a convex set")
       (names cauchy-convex-primitive-except cc-diff-on-of-eps-bound cc-continuous-at-add-const cauchy-convex-primitive)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 35) with the exceptional point, for a given base point o in u; the derivative of the primitive comes from the continuity of f alone through the new brick cc-diff-on-of-eps-bound (the converse of caratheodory-remainder-small). the exceptional-point hypothesis is the notes' (f continuous on the open set u, x in u, f holomorphic on u minus {x}), spelled with restrict(f, difference(u, singleton(x))) because HOLOMORPHIC-ON types its function on exactly its set. Earlier: PROVEN 2026-09-25 (batch 30) for f holomorphic on the whole convex open set U, for a given a0 in U (the notes' 'let a in U'): there is g in fun(U, CC), holomorphic on U, with is-diff-on(cc-normed-field, U, g, z, f(z)) at every z in U; the witness is the notes' (68), b |-> SEG-INT(f, a0, b), and its differentiability comes from a local quadratic bound (cc-diff-on-local-quadratic) obtained from the Caratheodory remainder at eps = 1. The exceptional point x as for 3.6.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose f is continuous on an open convex set U and holomorphic on U minus a point x. Then there is a holomorphic function g on U such that g' = f on U."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "3.9")
       (section "3.2" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Cauchy's theorem on a convex set")
       (names cauchy-convex-closed-except cauchy-convex-closed line-int-closed-road-of-derivative)
       (home-elsewhere line-int-closed-road-of-derivative)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-09-26 (batch 35) with the exceptional point; a closed path is a road on [a, b] with pgam(b) = pgam(a). the exceptional-point hypothesis is the notes' (f continuous on the open set u, x in u, f holomorphic on u minus {x}), spelled with restrict(f, difference(u, singleton(x))) because HOLOMORPHIC-ON types its function on exactly its set. Earlier: PROVEN 2026-09-25 (batch 30) for f holomorphic on the whole convex open set U: for a road on [a, b] with trace in U and gamma(b) = gamma(a), the integral of f along it is 0 (from 3.8 and Cor 3.3 for a closed road). 'Closed path' is the hypothesis gamma(b) = gamma(a); there is no closed-road predicate. The exceptional point x as for 3.6.")
       (notes-statement "If f is continuous on an open convex set U and holomorphic on U minus a point x, then for any closed path gamma in U the integral of f along gamma is 0."))

