       (notes-statement "The operator D : f |-> f' is continuous on H(U)."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "5.21")
       (section "5.3" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The seminorms of f' are bounded by seminorms of f")
       (names cc-deriv-seminorm-estimate cc-exhaust-ball-inside)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-14 on the exhaustion, with another constant: if |f| <= M on K_{4n+3} then |f'| <= 4(n+1) M on K_n, i.e. p_n(f') <= 4(n+1) p_{4n+3}(f). The notes bound p_K(f') through a triangle containing K (Cor. 3.42); here the bound comes from Cauchy's estimate on discs.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose conv{a, b, c} is contained in U and K is a compact subset of the interior of conv{a, b, c}. Then p_K(f') <= (1/2 pi) len(Delta[a,b,c]) (1 / inf{d(x) : x in K}^2) p_{Delta[a,b,c]*}(f), where d(x) is the distance from x to the triangle."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "5.22")
       (section "5.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Normally convergent series of holomorphic functions differentiate term by term")
       (names cc-series-holomorphic cc-derived-series-normally-convergent cc-normal-convergence-uniform cc-normal-convergence-pointwise cc-partial-sums-diff)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-14, with normal convergence on compact subsets stated as IS-NORMALLY-CONVERGENT on every K_n (equivalent by cc-exhaust-exhaustive): the derived series is normally convergent on every K_n, the sum S is holomorphic on u, and DERIV-ON(u, S, z) = CC-SERIES-LIMIT of the derivatives.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose the infinite sum sum_k f_k is normally convergent on compact subsets. Then sum_k D f_k is normally convergent on compact subsets and D(sum_k f_k) = sum_k D f_k."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "5.23")
       (section "5.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Hurwitz's theorem")
       (names cc-hurwitz cc-hurwitz-local)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-18. Convergence in H(U) is UNIF-ON-COMPACTA; 'f is a constant' is: some c in CC has f(z) = c for every z in u. The route differs from the notes: instead of the zero-counting integral of Corollary 4.21, which the library lacks, the zeros of f - a do not accumulate (2.12), |f - a| is bounded below on a small circle, and the mean value bound for 1/(f_k - a) gives the contradiction.")
       (notes-statement "Suppose U is a connected open set and {f_n} is a sequence of nowhere vanishing functions in H(U) which converges to f in the topology of H(U). Then either f is a constant or is nowhere vanishing."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
       (ref "5.24")
       (section "5.3" prefilled)
       (kind remark)
       (title "Hurwitz's theorem for a value a, and its contrapositive")
       (names cc-hurwitz-value cc-hurwitz-eventually)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "PROVEN 2026-10-18 by the route of 5.23 (no Corollary 4.21). The first sentence is stated for a sequence none of whose terms takes the value a; the statement for infinitely many n is stated in its contrapositive form, locally: if f is not constant and f(c) = a, then in every ball about c, f_k takes the value a for all large k.")
       (notes-statement "In Proposition 5.23 'nowhere vanishing' may be replaced by 'does not assume the value a'. If for infinitely many n, f_n does not assume the value a, then f does not assume the value a; contrapositively, if f assumes the value a, then for sufficiently large n, f_n assumes the value a."))

(entry (doc "complex-analysis")
