(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.1")
       (section "1.0" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Adjunction of a unit to a normed algebra")
       (names unitization-is-complex-normed-algebra unitization-unit-left unitization-unit-right unitization-norm-unit unitization-vnrm-submult unitization-vnrm-definite unitization-vnrm-homog unitization-vnrm-triangle unitization-vmul-assoc) ;; from-report: unitization-unit-left unitization-unit-right unitization-vnrm-submult unitization-vnrm-definite unitization-vnrm-homog unitization-vnrm-triangle unitization-vmul-assoc
       (status proven)
       (deviation "UNITIZATION-NA(a) on CC x A, scalar first, as the notes. Proven for a complex normed algebra a: IS-COMPLEX-NORMED-ALGEBRA(UNITIZATION-NA(a)); (1, 0) is a two-sided unit (unitization-unit-left / -right); ||(1, 0)|| = 1; the norm is definite, homogeneous, subadditive and submultiplicative. The unit is stated by those laws, since the vocabulary has no unital normed (non-complete) algebra structure.")
       (notes-statement "A complex normed algebra. On C x A with coordinatewise addition, (l, x)(r, y) = (l r, l y + r x + x y) and ||(l, x)|| = |l| + ||x||, the result is a normed algebra with unit (1, 0)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.3")
       (section "1.0" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Neumann series: 1 - x invertible for ||x|| < 1")
       (names neumann-series neumann-series-bound neumann-inverse-norm-bound)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The series is the sequence of partial sums NEUMANN-SUM(a, x, k) converging in BA-METRIC-SPACE(a) to INVERSE-EL(a, 1 - x). Bound (2) is stated as ||(1 - x)^(-1) - (1 + x)|| <= (||x|| ||x||) recip(1 - ||x||). Also proven: ||(1 - x)^(-1)|| <= recip(1 - ||x||).")
       (notes-statement "A Banach algebra with unit, ||x|| < 1. Then 1 - x is invertible, (1 - x)^(-1) = sum_(k >= 0) x^k, and ||(1 - x)^(-1) - (1 + x)|| <= ||x||^2 / (1 - ||x||)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.4")
       (section "1.0" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "The invertible elements form an open set")
       (names invertibles-open invertible-near-invertible)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Openness is IS-OPEN(BA-METRIC-SPACE(a), INVERTIBLES(a)); the quantitative half is stated with ||b - x|| < recip(||b^(-1)||).")
       (notes-statement "The set U of invertible elements of a Banach algebra with unit is open: if a in U and ||a - x|| < ||a^(-1)||^(-1) then x in U."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.5")
       (section "1.0" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Proper maximal left ideals are closed")
       (names maximal-left-ideal-closed left-ideal-closure commutative-maximal-ideal-closed)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for left ideals: IS-MAXIMAL-LEFT-IDEAL(a, I) implies IS-CLOSED(BA-METRIC-SPACE(a), I); left-ideal-closure: the closure of a left ideal is a left ideal. The two-sided maximal ideal of a commutative algebra is closed (commutative-maximal-ideal-closed). The statement for right ideals is not restated.")
       (notes-statement "In a Banach algebra with unit, a proper maximal left ideal is closed; likewise for right ideals."))

;;; ===== ch. 1 section 1: Gelfand-Mazur Theorem =====
(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.6")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The resolvent is analytic off the spectrum")
       (names resolvent-derivative resolvent-continuous resolvent-laurent resolvent-norm-bound spectrum-in-closed-ball)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Analyticity is norm differentiability, stated as an eps-delta remainder estimate: for u off sigma(x), z near u is off sigma(x) and ||F(z) - F(u) - (u - z) F(u)^2|| <= eps |z - u|, i.e. F'(u) = -F(u)^2; the tree has no vocabulary for holomorphy of an algebra-valued map. The Laurent series is stated as F(z) = lim_n z^(-1) sum_(k<n) (z^(-1) x)^k for |z| > ||x||; absolute convergence of sum z^(-(k+1)) x^k is not stated. The inclusion sigma(x) in B(0, ||x||) is spectrum-in-closed-ball. Also proven: ||F(z)|| <= 2 / |z| when |z| >= 2 ||x|| and |z| > 0.")
       (notes-statement "F(z) = (z 1 - a)^(-1) is defined and analytic on C \\ sigma(a); for |z| > ||a||, F(z) = sum_(k >= 0) z^(-(k+1)) a^k absolutely; and sigma(a) is contained in the closed ball B(0, ||a||)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.7")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "||a^n||^(1/n) converges (Fekete)")
       (names spectral-radius-limit fekete-lemma)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for a Banach algebra with unit; the sequence is indexed from 1 (n |-> ||x^(n+1)||^(1/(n+1))) and its limit is SPECTRAL-RADIUS(a, x) = inf_(n >= 1) ||x^n||^(1/n). fekete-lemma (library analysis) is the statement for a nonnegative submultiplicative real sequence, the limit characterised as the greatest lower bound of the roots.")
       (notes-statement "For a in A, the sequence ||a^n||^(1/n) is convergent (to inf_m ||a^m||^(1/m))."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.8")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The spectrum lies in the disc of radius rho(a)")
       (names spectrum-in-spectral-radius-disc spectral-radius-limit gelfand-spectral-radius-max)
       (home-elsewhere spectral-radius-limit)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for a unital Banach algebra, with SPECTRAL-RADIUS(a, x) = inf_(n >= 1) ||x^n||^(1/n), which is lim ||x^n||^(1/n) by 1.7 (spectral-radius-limit). The disc is B(0, rho(x)). Also proven (gelfand-spectral-radius-max): rho(x) = max |sigma(x)|.")
       (notes-statement "For any a in A, sigma(a) is contained in the closed ball B(0, rho(a)), rho(a) = lim_k ||a^k||^(1/k)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.9")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The spectrum is nonempty")
       (names spectrum-nonempty)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated for every element x of a unital Banach algebra: FORSOME z, z in SPECTRUM(a, x). Route: complex Hahn-Banach norming functional (cc-norming-functional), holomorphy of psi o resolvent, Liouville.")
       (notes-statement "In a Banach algebra (with unit), sigma(a) is nonempty for every a in A."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.10")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Gelfand-Mazur")
       (names gelfand-mazur gelfand-mazur-from-nonempty-spectrum scalar-embedding-injective)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "The field hypothesis is read as: every nonzero element of the unital Banach algebra is invertible. Proven: every x equals z 1 for some z in CC, and z |-> z 1 is injective. Commutativity is not used. The algebra isomorphism (IS-ALG-HOM of z |-> z 1 onto A) is not stated.")
       (notes-statement "A commutative Banach algebra with unit which is a field is algebraically isomorphic to C."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.11")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "A / M is C for a maximal ideal M")
       (names maximal-quotient-field maximal-quotient-scalar maximal-quotient-scalar-unique maximal-ideal-character commutative-maximal-ideal-closed)
       (home-elsewhere commutative-maximal-ideal-closed)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Stated through the quotient QUOTIENT-BA(a, M): every coset is z 1 + M for a unique z in CC (maximal-quotient-scalar, -unique) and A/M is a field (maximal-quotient-field); the isomorphism is packaged as the character chi with ker chi = M (maximal-ideal-character). M is closed by commutative-maximal-ideal-closed.")
       (notes-statement "If M is a maximal ideal in a commutative complex Banach algebra A with unit, A/M is algebraically isomorphic to C."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.14")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The characters form a weak* closed set")
       (names characters-closed-in-product gelfand-space-compact)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "The weak* topology is the topology of pointwise convergence: CHARACTERS(a) is closed in TPRODUCT-TOP(VEC a, x |-> METRIC-TOP CC-MS), not in A*; the characters are bounded (character-bounded), so this is the same set. Commutativity is not used; stated for a unital Banach algebra. GELFAND-SPACE(a) is compact (Tychonoff on the product of the discs |z| <= ||x||).")
       (notes-statement "For a commutative Banach algebra A, the set of multiplicative unit-preserving linear functionals A -> C is a w<A*, A> closed subset of A*."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.15")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The Gelfand transform is a homomorphism of norm 1")
       (names gelfand-transform-mul gelfand-transform-add gelfand-transform-act gelfand-transform-one gelfand-transform-bounded gelfand-transform-continuous gelfand-transform-in-ccont) ;; from-report: gelfand-transform-mul gelfand-transform-add gelfand-transform-act gelfand-transform-one gelfand-transform-bounded
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Proven: the homomorphism laws pointwise at each character, |G(x)(chi)| <= ||x||, and G(x) in CCONT(GELFAND-SPACE a) for every x. Not stated: G as an IS-ALG-HOM into CCONT-ALGEBRA(GELFAND-SPACE a), and the operator norm of G equal to 1.")
       (notes-statement "The Gelfand transform G : A -> C(A-hat) is an algebra homomorphism, bounded of norm 1."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.16")
       (section "1.1" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The range of G(a) is the spectrum; ||G(a)||sup = rho(a)")
       (names gelfand-range-spectrum gelfand-sup-spectral-radius)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "For a commutative unital Banach algebra: SPECTRUM(a, x) = IMAGE(G(x), CHARACTERS(a)) (gelfand-range-spectrum) and SUP-NORM-T over GELFAND-SPACE(a) of G(x) equals SPECTRAL-RADIUS(a, x) (gelfand-sup-spectral-radius).")
       (notes-statement "The range of G(a) is sigma(a); in particular ||G(a)||_sup = rho(a)."))

;;; ===== ch. 1 section 2: Involution =====
(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.18")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The unitization of an involutive algebra is involutive")
       (names unitization-is-involutive-banach-algebra unitization-is-unital-banach-algebra unitization-complete)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "A is a NU-INVOLUTIVE-BANACH-ALGEBRA (involution accessor ISTAR); the result is IS-UNITAL-INVOLUTIVE-BANACH-ALGEBRA(UNITIZATION-STAR(a)), a 10-tuple on CC x A, scalar first. Completeness of CC x A under the sum norm is by comparison with the max metric of PRODUCT2-MS (unitization-complete).")
       (notes-statement "If A is an involutive Banach algebra, the unitization A~ is an involutive Banach algebra with (l, x)* = (conj l, x*)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.19")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "A unique C* norm on the unitization")
       (names unitization-cstar-norm-unique)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Uniqueness only: for any a and norms nra, nrb, if both unitization 10-tuples (with norm slot nra, resp. nrb) are unital C*-algebras, then nra = nrb on UNIT-VEC(a). No hypothesis on a is needed. Existence of a C* norm on the unitization is not proven.")
       (notes-statement "If A is a C*-algebra, there is a unique norm on A~ making it a C*-algebra."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.22")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Characters of a commutative C*-algebra preserve the involution")
       (names character-preserves-involution ccsa-character-preserves-involution)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "Also proven for any unital C*-algebra (character-preserves-involution): commutativity is not used. Route: chi(h) is in sigma(h) and sigma(h) is real for self-adjoint h (bcp-sa-spectrum-real), then x = (h1 - i h2)/2 with h1, h2 self-adjoint; not the |omega(x + i t)|^2 argument of the notes.")
       (notes-statement "If A is a commutative C*-algebra and omega a unit-preserving multiplicative linear functional, then omega(x*) = conj(omega(x))."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.23")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind theorem)
       (title "Commutative Gelfand-Naimark")
       (names gelfand-isometric gelfand-transform-in-ccont gelfand-sup-spectral-radius)
       (home-elsewhere gelfand-transform-in-ccont gelfand-sup-spectral-radius)
       (status partial)
       (deviation "Proven: G maps into CCONT(GELFAND-SPACE a) and is isometric (SUP-NORM-T(GELFAND-SPACE a, G x) = ||x||), by ||x||^2 = ||G(x* x)|| <= ||G x*|| ||G x|| <= ||x|| ||G x||; the homomorphism laws are gelfand-transform-add / -act / -mul / -one. Not stated: G(x*) = conj G(x) as a theorem on the transform (character-preserves-involution is the pointwise fact), and surjectivity onto C(A-hat) (Stone-Weierstrass, closed range).")
       (notes-statement "If A is a commutative C*-algebra with unit, the Gelfand transform is an isometric involution-preserving isomorphism A -> C(A-hat)."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.24")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "Unital *-homomorphisms of C*-algebras have norm 1")
       (names cstar-hom-contractive cstar-hom-norm-one)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "'Continuous of norm 1' is stated as ||f(x)|| <= ||x|| for all x together with ||f(1)|| = 1; the operator norm of f is not a defined object. The homomorphism is IS-STAR-HOM (IS-ALG-HOM, which includes f(1) = 1, plus f(x*) = f(x)*). The first step, rho(f x) <= rho(x), holds for any unital algebra homomorphism of unital Banach algebras (hom-spectral-radius-le).")
       (notes-statement "A, B C*-algebras with unit; a unit and involution preserving homomorphism phi : A -> B is continuous of norm 1."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.25")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind corollary)
       (title "At most one C* norm")
       (names cstar-norm-unique)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "'The same involutive algebra with two norms' is two unital C*-algebras a, c whose VEC, VADD, ACT, VMUL, VONE and VSTAR agree slot by slot (==); the conclusion is ||x||_c = ||x||_a for all x in VEC(a).")
       (notes-statement "An involutive algebra with unit has at most one norm making it a C*-algebra."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.26")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The dual map of Gelfand spaces is continuous")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Needs GELFAND-SPACE (exists) and 1.24 (proven: cstar-hom-contractive); continuity into a subspace of a product is the tree's tprod-hom-iff.")
       (notes-statement "A, B commutative C*-algebras with unit, phi : A -> B unit and involution preserving. Then phi-hat : B-hat -> A-hat, omega |-> omega o phi, is continuous."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.28")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuous maps of Gelfand spaces come from homomorphisms")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Needs 1.23 (surjectivity of G is not proven) and 1.26; the 'injective iff surjective' clause needs Urysohn on a compact Hausdorff space or Stone-Weierstrass.")
       (notes-statement "Any continuous g : B-hat -> A-hat is phi-hat for a unique unit-preserving homomorphism phi : A -> B; phi is injective iff phi-hat is surjective."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.29")
       (section "1.2" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "The spectrum in a C*-subalgebra is absolute")
       (names spectral-permanence)
       (status proven)
       (deviation "A unital C*-subalgebra is the relation IS-UNITAL-C-STAR-SUBALGEBRA(a, b): both are unital C*-algebras, VEC(a) is a subset of VEC(b), the units agree, and every operation of a is the restriction of b's. The proof is not the notes' reduction to C(X): the spectrum of a self-adjoint element is real (bcp-sa-spectrum-real), a self-adjoint element invertible in B is invertible in A, and a general element goes through y*y and yy*.")
       (notes-statement "A contained in B, C*-algebras with unit, 1_B in A. The spectrum of a in A relative to A equals its spectrum relative to B."))

;;; ===== ch. 1 section 3: Functional Calculus =====
(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.31")
       (section "1.3" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Continuous functional calculus for a normal element")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Only the vocabulary of the C*-algebra generated by an element exists: CSTAR-GEN(a, x), the intersection of the norm-closed unital *-subalgebras containing x, with cstar-gen-contains, cstar-gen-least, cstar-gen-subset. The calculus waits for 1.23 (surjectivity of G).")
       (notes-statement "A a C*-algebra, a normal. There is a unique involution and unit preserving homomorphism F_a : C(sigma(a)) -> A with F_a(h) = a, h(z) = z."))

;;; ===== ch. 1 section 4: Ordering =====
(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.32")
       (section "1.4" prefilled)
       (kind lemma)
       (title "The binomial series for sqrt converges absolutely at 1")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "The coefficients binom(1/2, k) are not formed (binomial.scm has natural-number binomials); series-abs-converges and the comparison tests exist.")
       (notes-statement "The series sum_(k >= 1) (3/2 - 1)(3/2 - 2) ... (3/2 - k) / k! of real numbers is absolutely convergent."))

(entry (doc "banach-algebras")
       (ref "1.33")
       (section "1.4" prefilled)
       (kind proposition)
       (title "Square root of 1 + a for ||a|| <= 1")
       (names)
       (status absent)
       (deviation "Needs 1.32, summable families in the algebra (absolute-summable-implies-summable) and SA-PART. Stated for a COMPLEX unital Banach algebra if real scalars are excluded (decision D1); the notes state it for a real one.")
       (notes-statement "A a real Banach algebra, ||a|| <= 1. Then 1 + sum_k binom(1/2, k) a^k converges absolutely to b with b^2 = 1 + a; if a commutes with x so does b; if A is complex involutive and a in A_sa, b in A_sa."))

